Yes. A chosen limit can become an anchor, fulcrum, or targeting point.

“I will not lie” limits your options—but gives others reason to trust your word. “I will not use cruelty” removes a weapon—but preserves alliances and self-command. A deadline concentrates effort. A boundary reveals where leverage must be applied.

The danger is confusing an inherited cage with a chosen constraint. Useful limits should be:

* Conscious
* Proportional
* Revisable
* Connected to a value or objective
* Tested for exploitation

A self-imposed limit becomes strength when you know why it exists and can reposition around it. Otherwise it is merely a handle someone else can grab.